WEBVTT

00:00:00.025 --> 00:00:04.502
A practical selection workflow begins by defining the required output.

00:00:04.502 --> 00:00:11.017
Do you need one label for the whole document, labels for individual tokens, generated text,

00:00:11.017 --> 00:00:17.047
an embedding vector, a relevance score, or an answer grounded in external documents?

00:00:17.047 --> 00:00:20.167
That answer narrows the architecture family.

00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:23.652
Next check language and domain.

00:00:23.652 --> 00:00:28.552
A strong English model may be a poor choice for multilingual data,

00:00:28.552 --> 00:00:34.107
and a general-domain model may miss specialized clinical or legal terminology.

00:00:34.107 --> 00:00:41.247
Then check constraints: available RAM or GPU memory, latency, cost, privacy requirements, license,

00:00:41.247 --> 00:00:43.432
and where the model must run.

00:00:43.432 --> 00:00:50.107
Finally, test a small set of realistic examples before committing to a large experiment.

00:00:50.107 --> 00:00:56.982
Ten or twenty carefully chosen examples can reveal tokenization problems, domain mismatch,

00:00:56.982 --> 00:01:01.127
unexpected output formats, or unacceptable latency.

00:01:01.127 --> 00:01:04.382
Popularity is not a substitute for fit.

00:01:04.382 --> 00:01:07.920
Choose a model because it matches the task and constraints.
